### What is edit distance?

Edit distance, also called Levenshtein distance, measures how different two strings are by counting the minimum number of single-character operations needed to transform one string into the other. The three allowed operations are:

- **Insert** a character into string A.
- **Delete** a character from string A.
- **Replace** a character in string A with a different character.

For example, transforming "KITTEN" into "SITTING" requires three operations: replace K with S, replace E with I, insert G at the end.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EditDistanceBasic {

    public static int editDistance(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        // Base cases: transforming a prefix to/from empty string
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;  // i deletions
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;  // j insertions

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];  // match: no operation needed
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(
                        dp[i - 1][j - 1],  // replace
                        Math.min(dp[i - 1][j],   // delete from A
                                 dp[i][j - 1])   // insert into A
                    );
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(editDistance("RAJHES", "RAJESH"));  // 2
        System.out.println(editDistance("KITTEN", "SITTING")); // 3
    }
}
```

```text
2
3
```

### Why does edit distance exist?

Edit distance exists because exact string matching is too rigid for real-world data. Account holder names in banking systems have typos, abbreviations, and transliteration differences. A customer named RAJESH might appear as RAJHES, RAJSH, or RAJESSH in different records. Edit distance quantifies how close two strings are, enabling fuzzy matching with a threshold.

It is also the foundation of diff tools, spell-checkers, DNA sequence alignment, and plagiarism detection.

### How does the DP table work internally?

The table has (m + 1) rows and (n + 1) columns, where m and n are the lengths of the two strings. Cell dp[i][j] represents the edit distance between the first i characters of A and the first j characters of B.

The first column dp[i][0] is i, because transforming a prefix of length i into an empty string requires i deletions. The first row dp[0][j] is j, because building a prefix of length j from an empty string requires j insertions.

For the body, when `A[i-1] == B[j-1]`, the current characters match and no operation is needed, so dp[i][j] equals the diagonal dp[i-1][j-1]. When they differ, we take 1 plus the minimum of three neighbours:

- dp[i-1][j-1] represents **replace**: change A[i-1] to B[j-1] and solve the rest.
- dp[i-1][j] represents **delete**: remove A[i-1] and match the remaining prefix.
- dp[i][j-1] represents **insert**: insert B[j-1] into A and match the remaining suffix.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EditDistanceTablePrint {

    public static void printTable(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? dp[i - 1][j - 1]
                    : 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j - 1], Math.min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]));

        // Print the table
        System.out.print("    ");
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++)
            System.out.printf("%3s", j == 0 ? "_" : String.valueOf(b.charAt(j - 1)));
        System.out.println();
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            System.out.printf("%3s", i == 0 ? "_" : String.valueOf(a.charAt(i - 1)));
            for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++)
                System.out.printf("%3d", dp[i][j]);
            System.out.println();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printTable("ACE", "ABCE");
    }
}
```

