### When should you use edit distance in banking?

Edit distance is used in KYC (Know Your Customer) name matching. When a customer submits a name that does not exactly match the official record, the system computes the edit distance. If the distance is below a threshold (for example, two edits for names under ten characters), the system flags it as a likely typo rather than a different person.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class KycNameMatcher {

    public static boolean isFuzzyMatch(String submitted, String official, int threshold) {
        int distance = editDistance(submitted.toUpperCase(), official.toUpperCase());
        return distance <= threshold;
    }

    private static int editDistance(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[] prev = new int[n + 1], curr = new int[n + 1];
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) prev[j] = j;
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            curr[0] = i;
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                curr[j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? prev[j - 1]
                    : 1 + Math.min(prev[j - 1], Math.min(prev[j], curr[j - 1]));
            int[] t = prev; prev = curr; curr = t;
        }
        return prev[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isFuzzyMatch("RAJHES", "RAJESH", 2));    // true
        System.out.println(isFuzzyMatch("PANKAJ", "RAJESH", 2));    // false
        System.out.println(isFuzzyMatch("RAJESSH", "RAJESH", 1));   // true
    }
}
